California is the country's largest  agricultural producer it's also running  out of water and that could have a big  impact on food prices this chart shows  how drought conditions have worsened  over the last four years more than forty  percent of the state is now in  exceptional drought and that could be a  problem for the whole country but eighty  percent of California's water is used  for agriculture it's a nearly 50 billion  dollar industry that produces dairy beef  eggs fruits nuts and vegetables more  than ninety percent of the country  strawberries figs grapes lemons  nectarines olives and plums are all  grown in California and it produces  about ninety percent of the country's  tree nuts most of these crops are grown  in the Central Valley a desert region  where the soil is great for growing but  requires heavy irrigation consider this  it takes about a gallon of water to  produce a single almond California  agriculture relies on snowpack in the  Sierra Nevada mountains but the snow is  at the lowest level in recorded history  the most recent measurement shows  snowpack ninety-five percent lower than  what's normal for this time of year the  state's reservoirs are also dangerously  low these images from NASA show the  Shasta Lake Reservoir California's  largest water levels have decreased  fifty percent since 2011 and with less  water consumers across the country could  pay more fruit and vegetable prices are  expected to rise about three percent  this year partially because of the  ongoing drought if conditions don't  improve soon this may only be the  beginning  Oh
 

